Package: phylloptim
Title: Leaf Gas Exchange with Explicit Plant Hydraulics
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Isaac", "Towers", , "", "aut",
            comment = "Author of the leaf gas exchange and hydraulics model"),
    person("Daniel", "Falster", , "daniel.falster@unsw.edu.au", c("aut", "cre"),
            comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-9814-092X")),
    person("Rich", "FitzJohn", , "", "ctb",
            comment = "Root-finding utilities (uniroot.hpp)")
    )
Maintainer: Daniel Falster <daniel.falster@unsw.edu.au>
Description: An implementation of leaf-level gas exchange in which stomatal
    behaviour emerges from hydraulics rather than an empirical conductance
    model. Farquhar-von Caemmerer-Berry photosynthesis is coupled to an explicit
    soil-root-stem-leaf water transport path, with Weibull vulnerability curves
    for both xylem and roots, multi-layer soil and gravitational head, and the
    operating point is chosen by Sperry-style gain-risk profit maximisation over
    the root-collar water potential. Forward-mode automatic differentiation
    supplies exact derivatives of profit with respect to the collar potential.
    Extracted from the TF24 strategy in the 'plant' package so that it can be
    tested, profiled, extended and embedded independently. The model itself is a
    set of self-contained C++ headers, under inst/include, that use no R and no
    'Rcpp' and depend only on Boost and the header-only parts of 'odelia': they
    compile and run with no R present at all, so the model can be linked
    directly into a C++ program or bound to another language such as Python
    without going through this package. R users get the same model through an
    'RcppR6' layer that sits on top of those headers and is never included by
    them, and R packages get it by naming 'phylloptim' in their LinkingTo field.
